2022-Feb-18  This is currently not working.  The biggest problem is that
  Asymptote has trouble with GhostScript, and while they are working that
  through, I am using the graphic files rather than the sources.
  There are other, more minor issues. 

========================================
2022 Notes

-1) Comment: there has been an issue between Asymptote and Ghostscript.  There
  was some kind of vulnerability in GS and they made some changes, and
  the ASY version does not work with the GS version.  I am sure that this
  will all work great someday, but as of today (2021-June-08) it can't
  make the 3d.asy file without by-hand compilation of at least one of the
  two programs.  I instead added a number of files, such as
  src/background/asy/3d/3d000.pdf to the repo as a temp patch.  This explains
  step (2).

0) Fresh Ubuntu 20 install, from scratch.  Fresh TeX Live, from the apt
package texlive-full.

1) In a terminal clone the git repo.
  $ cd ~/Documents
  $ git clone https://gitlab.com/jim.hefferon/toc.git computing

2) Get Asymptote to run.
  a) Make sure that imagemagick is installed.
  b) In /etc/ImageMagick-6/policy.xml change lines at the bottom
    about PS, PDF, etc from rights="none" to rights="read|write".
  c) Put the following lines at the end of your .profile in your home
  directory (change "/Documents/computing" to wherever you put the repo).

# Directories for the Asymptote drawing program
export ASYMPTOTE_DIR=$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy:$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy/share:$HOME/Documents/computing/src/asy/asy-graphtheory-master/modules:$ASYMPTOTE_DIR

  Log out and log in again (this runs the profile).  You can check that the
  prior works by opening a terminal and running "echo $ASYMPTOTE_DIR".
  
3) Run make.
  $ cd ~/Documents/computing/src
  $ make
Wait.

  